from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate,Paragraph,Spacer,Table,TableStyle,KeepTogether
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet,ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.enums import TA_CENTER
import csv
p='/mnt/data/fair_affine_comparison.pdf'; doc=SimpleDocTemplate(p,pagesize=(595,842),leftMargin=60,rightMargin=60,topMargin=58,bottomMargin=58)
s=getSampleStyleSheet();s.add(ParagraphStyle(name='HeadingX',parent=s['Heading2'],spaceBefore=14,spaceAfter=7));s.add(ParagraphStyle(name='BodyX',parent=s['BodyText'],leading=15,spaceAfter=9));s.add(ParagraphStyle(name='SmallX',parent=s['BodyText'],fontSize=8.5,leading=12,spaceAfter=8)); story=[]
def para(t,style='BodyX'):story.append(Paragraph(t,s[style]))
para('A Fair Comparison of Physical Affine and Modal Enclosures','Title');para('Correlation Preservation in a Reduced Stuart–Landau Resource Model','HeadingX');para('Flyxion | November 2026','SmallX')
para('<b>Abstract.</b> A previously reported improvement from collective–disagreement coordinates is reexamined against a physical-coordinate representation that preserves exactly the same initial uncertainty. The two correlated representations are algebraically equivalent and yield identical interval enclosures when their propagation arithmetic is conjugate. The improvement relative to independent physical-coordinate boxes therefore measures the cost of losing correlations, not a coordinate-intrinsic superiority.')
para('1. Reduced nonlinear system','HeadingX')
para('For two real oscillators, u and v, with a shared uncertain growth parameter a, fixed coupling k, and cumulative actuator expenditure J, consider u\' = au - u³ - k(u-v), v\' = av - v³ + k(u-v), and J\' = 2 c_A k² (u-v)². Let p=(u+v)/2 and m=(u-v)/2. Then p\' = ap - p³ - 3pm², m\' = (a-2k)m - 3p²m - m³, and J\' = 8 c_A k² m².')
para('2. Representation-equivalence proposition','HeadingX')
para('<b>Proposition.</b> Let Y be a validated interval enclosure in (p,m,J) coordinates, and define the correlated physical affine enclosure C(Y) = {(p+m,p-m,J) : (p,m,J) belongs to Y}. If a physical affine propagator evaluates the transformed vector field using the same interval operations and the same rounding decisions as the modal propagator, and then applies C only when reporting physical-coordinate bounds, its output equals C(Y) at every checkpoint.')
para('<b>Proof.</b> The map C is an invertible linear transformation of the two state coordinates. The physical vector field is the conjugate of the modal vector field under C. Therefore transporting the shared generators p and m through the transformed flow and applying C at output gives precisely the same set enclosure as transporting them in the modal representation. The assertion is equality of the specified computational constructions, not equality of every possible interval algorithm in the two coordinate systems.')
para('3. Validated numerical comparison','HeadingX')
para('The experiment uses a in [0.15,0.19], k=0.7, c_A=0.1, p(0) in [0.329,0.331], m(0) in [0.119,0.121], J(0)=0, and a time step of 0.01. The algorithm checks a local Picard enclosure before each update, using exact rational interval operations and 40-bit outward dyadic endpoint rounding. Direct physical-coordinate intervals begin from the hull of the same correlated initial set.')
rows=list(csv.DictReader(open('/mnt/data/fair_affine_comparison.csv')))
data=[['Horizon','Direct width','Correlated width','Direct J upper','Correlated J upper']]
for r in rows[1:]:data.append([f"{float(r['horizon']):.1f}",f"{float(r['coordinate_hull_width']):.6f}",f"{float(r['physical_affine_width']):.6f}",f"{float(r['coordinate_hull_J_upper']):.6f}",f"{float(r['physical_affine_J_upper']):.6f}"])
t=Table(data,colWidths=[57,99,105,100,110],repeatRows=1);t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#eeeeee')),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),0.3,colors.grey),('FONTSIZE',(0,0),(-1,-1),8),('ALIGN',(1,1),(-1,-1),'RIGHT'),('VALIGN',(0,0),(-1,-1),'MIDDLE'),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7)]));story.append(t)
para('At H=0.8, the correlated width is 0.09197746 versus 0.13088858 for the independent-coordinate hull, a reduction of 29.73%. The correlated expenditure upper bound is 0.00202269 versus 0.00236184. Physical affine and modal calculations coincide because the same correlated enclosure is used.','BodyX')
para('4. Resource-floor implication and limitations','HeadingX')
para('For E(t)=E(0)+(q-b)t-J(t), take E(0)=0.1, q=0, b=0.005, and a floor E*=0.08. Since J is nondecreasing and b is nonnegative, the final upper bound on J also gives an all-prefix lower bound E(t) >= 0.1 - 0.005H - J_upper(H). At H=0.8 this is 0.09397731, above the floor. This conclusion applies to the reduced real-state model and stated parameter interval.')
para('The experiment does not demonstrate a genuinely moving Lohner frame, nor does it compare different optimal frame-selection algorithms. The physical affine representation is implemented by retaining the shared p and m generators and conjugating their enclosure at output; it is intentionally algebraically equivalent to the modal implementation. It establishes a controlled baseline for subsequent tests of nonlinear frame reconditioning. The dyadic inclusion procedure gives conservative enclosures under the stated exact-rational Picard and rounding operations; it does not certify the full complex heterogeneous-frequency network.')
para('5. Next obligation','HeadingX')
para('A genuine frame-comparison experiment should transport a time-dependent linear frame A(t), certify its inverse and nonlinear remainder, and compare the resulting reachable-set hull against a fixed affine frame under identical initial generators, time steps, and rounding precision. Only such a comparison can isolate the effect of dynamic reconditioning.')
doc.build(story);print(p)
